§9.2: a quote has **no head of its own**. The only way to configure one is the
**decorator line** directly above it (§9.1, a line touches the `>` it decorates):

@@{.outer}
> a plain quoted body

A claimed `type` renders its component, and every extra lands on the quote node
(§11 rule 3):

@@quoteA{.q, #q1}
> quoted *text*

An unclaimed type falls back to the built-in `<blockquote>` and still keeps every
extra (§11 rule 3, D8):

@@quoteZ{.z}
> an unclaimed type

Anything written **inside** the `>` is content, not a head: `@@` and the braces
are literal text there, and the body keeps the constructs and slots that its own
elements claim (§9.2):

> @@quoteA{.q} this is not a head
